    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21683",
      "problem": "Given the set of functions $\\{ e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ explain why they are linearly independent.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the set of functions $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ Otherwise, if $k_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that \n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{0})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)=0 \\\\  & {k}_{1}\\cdot 1+{k}_{2}\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,{k}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {k}_{1}({e}^{1})+{k}_{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)=0 \\\\  & e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\,⇒\\,e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=0 \\\\ e{k}_{1}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡1)\\,{k}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}.$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}.$",
